INT. STRANDED COACH BUS - 3 A.M.

A long-distance bus at the roadside, hazard lights ticking amber against a field of black. Rows of reclining seats, empty. A fine layer of frost on the inner windows. The driver's seat is vacant, door gaping open to the night.

NAOMI PRESCOTT, 30s, parka and wool cap, sits in the third row from the back with a paperback she isn't reading. Across the aisle, EMERY LUND, 20s, headphones, hoodie, bounces one knee.

Neither has spoken for twenty minutes.

EMERY
(to the bus, loud)
Hello? Anyone else on here?

Silence. Hazards tick.

NAOMI
There's nobody else.

EMERY
Right.

NAOMI
The driver stepped out for the radio.

EMERY
That was an hour ago.

NAOMI
Forty minutes.

EMERY
(muttering)
Felt like an hour.

She pulls the headphones down around her neck. Naomi turns a page. Doesn't read it. The bus creaks, settling on its springs.

EMERY (CONT'D)
Did you hear that?

NAOMI
Hear what?

EMERY
Like tapping.

NAOMI
It's the cold.

EMERY
It's not the cold.

NAOMI
It's the cold.

Tap. Tap. Tap-tap.

Both sit very still. The sound comes from somewhere in the back of the bus. Not the engine. Not the heater. Something closer, patient.

EMERY
It's in the luggage rack.

NAOMI
Don't.

EMERY
Someone left a bag.

NAOMI
Emery.

EMERY
I'm going to look.

She stands. Naomi stands too, in the same moment, then sits back down, and then stands again. They both end up in the aisle, and the bus sways, and neither wants to take the first step.

EMERY (CONT'D)
(to Naomi, whispering)
You first.

NAOMI
(to the bus)
Whoever's there, we know you're in there.

EMERY ^
Whoever's there, we know you're in there.

They say it at the same time. They look at each other.

NAOMI
Stop that.

EMERY
You stop.

The tapping stops. Instead, a soft, wet noise, like a hand pulled slowly out of mud.

The overhead luggage rack shudders. A zipper rasps. A black duffel bag unzips by itself, one slow tooth at a time.

Naomi clutches the seat. Emery clutches Naomi's arm. They back up the aisle together, step for step, until they hit the rear emergency door.

NAOMI (CONT'D)
(whispering)
Open it.

EMERY
It's locked.

NAOMI
Open it!

EMERY
It's a lever! It's on the floor!

She drops. Gropes under the seat. Naomi keeps her eyes on the duffel. The zipper rasps another inch.

EMERY (CONT'D)
(to herself)
Come on. Come on.

Something pale slides out of the bag. Fingers, knuckled and long. They curl around the edge of the rack.

NAOMI
(very low)
Emery. Pull it. Now.

Emery pulls. The emergency door swings out with a clatter, and cold air slams into the bus. The hazards tick. The fingers withdraw.

EMERY
Go! Go!

Naomi shoves her out the door, tumbles after. They land in the gravel on their hands and knees, gasping.

Behind them, the bus sits glowing amber, silent. The duffel, lying on the aisle floor, is zipped shut again.

NAOMI
(panting)
Don't look back.

EMERY
I'm not.

NAOMI
Good.

They crawl away from the road. Neither looks. Both hear it: the soft tap of a hand on the glass.
